<v Liam>Wait a minute — once cells connect into multicellular filaments like cyanobacteria in Rung CL03 and share cytoplasm through fifteen-nanometer SepJ nanopores, doesn't one punctured cell drain the whole hundred-cell filament dry?

7
00:02:03.380 --> 00:02:24.335
<v Kenji>It absolutely does if the pore stays open! In model CA-M2, an ungated twenty-cell filament collapses to zero percent survival when cell-zero lyses. With calcium-triggered SepJ plug closure in fifty milliseconds, nineteen out of twenty cells — ninety-five percent — survive intact.

8
00:02:24.525 --> 00:02:39.730
<v Liam>And I love that Section 3 refuses to repeat romanticized pop-science myths! People constantly claim forest mother trees broadcast intentional warning messages to kin through fungal networks, and we actually checked the forestry audit.

9
00:02:40.000 --> 00:03:03.148
<v Cecily>Yes! Simard's 1997 carbon-isotope transfer across ectomycorrhizal hyphae is solid biophysics — tagged E — but Karst, Jones, and Hoeksema's 2023 systematic audit exposed heavy citation bias and confounders behind teleological mother-tree stories, so we separate biophysical flux from unsubstantiated signaling intent.

10
00:03:03.418 --> 00:03:18.509
<v Cecily>Now map that exact same gate principle onto seventy-six years of artificial intelligence from 1950 to 2026! Why did AI Winter One hit in 1974 and AI Winter Two hit in 1988?

11
00:03:18.779 --> 00:03:48.896
<v Kenji>Every surge paired a compact externalized representation with a sound gate — Lisp recursion in 1956, backprop chain-rule gradients in 1986, sparse MoE top-k routing in 2022. Every AI Winter struck when signals outran verification: Hearsay-II untyped blackboards locked up quadratically, and MYCIN certainty factors drifted across multi-hop chains.

